window.docContents[30].resolve({"/Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes/#using-the-library":{"contents":"The preceding chapters develop the calculus. This one is about working in it: the annotations an\nauthor writes, what each one changes, and the indexes the library generates from them.\n\nFour things cannot be inferred from a definition and so must be stated by its author. Each is a\nfamily of annotations, and each has its own section below.\n\n* Where the calculus is left — each site at which a value already carrying one kind is re-typed\nas another, the plumbing that must drop to the carrier, the point at which a kinded value becomes\na naked one. The\nboundary family: @[kindCrossing], @[kindIngest], @[kindConst],\n@[carrierVocab] and @[kindEmission] each name the tier that sanctions a boundary site;\n@[kindCarrier] is what brings a downstream carrier under the audit at all, @[kindAttest]\nregisters a wrapper through which authored mints go on the record, and\n@[kindCounterexample] exempts a deliberate falsification probe from the provenance sweeps.* What a vocabulary answers for — whether every dimension-one kind is individuated, every\nboundary carries a measurement model, every inversion says where it fails. The\ncensus marks: @[kindPrincipleFree], @[kindRelationFree] and\n@[kindInversionTotal] record an honest negative with its reason, and @[kindDiagnostic]\nenrolls a conditioning output.* How a definition should read — the notation, the constants, the intermediate bindings a rendered\nequation should keep. The rendering family: @[pkc_math], steered by\n@[pkc_math_symbol], @[pkc_math_config] and @[pkc_math_transparent].* What an external document says — which requirement a theorem discharges, which clause of\nDybkær or VIM a definition formalizes. The metadata family:\n@[requirement], @[dybkaer] and @[vim4].\n\nThe distinction that matters most is between the first two families and the other two, and it is a\ndistinction about what happens when an annotation is absent. A boundary site that carries no tier\nis reported as a violation by #kind_boundary_audit, and that report is pinned, so omitting\n@[kindCrossing] fails the build, and a dimension-one kind with neither a principle nor a\n@[kindPrincipleFree] mark fails #kind_examination_clean the same way: the annotation discharges\nan obligation about the code, in the way a proof discharges one about a proposition. Omitting a rendering or metadata annotation fails\nnothing — @[pkc_math_symbol] changes only how something reads. Read the Enforcement column below\nwith that asymmetry in mind: it says checked for @[pkc_math] and @[pkc_math_config] too, but\nwhat those checks reject is a misuse of the annotation — an argument naming a binding the\ndefinition does not have, an attribute attached to something that is not a structure — never its\nabsence.\n\nEverything in the Generated indexes section at the end is computed from the environment when this\nchapter is built, scoped to this package's own worked examples. No row is maintained by hand, so a\nrenamed declaration is a build error rather than a stale table.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty","header":"33. Using the library: annotations and generated indexes","id":"/Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes/#using-the-library"},"/Write-Once___-Correctly/Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum--Julia___-emitted-___-the-sealed-algorithm-handed-to-a-host-that-cannot-call-a-theorem":{"contents":"The strategies above keep the model inside Lean: every carrier is a Lean type, and the generated\nCUDA is launched from a Lean program. A model's consumer is often elsewhere. An Earth-system model\nwritten in Julia, a calibration pipeline, an analysis notebook can call a function it is handed; it\ncannot instantiate a [NumCarrier α] kernel or cite a theorem. The Julia emitter\n(PropertyKindCalculus.Torch.Paradigm.TapeCodegenJulia) is how a sealed algorithm reaches such a\nhost: the same recorded, compacted tape the megakernel is lowered from is rendered as a Julia\nfunction — a plain host loop, the bit-exact twin, and a KernelAbstractions.jl @kernel the one .jl\nruns on CPU, CUDA, AMDGPU or Metal, the portability twin, which is tolerance-only because a backend may\nfuse a·b + c into a multiply-add (the CPU backend does, through PoCL) and a conditioned tail — the LM\nstep's normal equations and Cholesky solve — amplifies that ulp a thousandfold while leaving the relative\nerror near 1e-13. It is not a fifth carrier. It is the fourth strategy's intermediate representation\ngiven a third surface syntax, beside the CUDA kernel and the C stub, so that what crosses into the\nhost is the tape and nothing else.\n\nThat last clause is the strategy. It rests on a theorem and on a reading of the emitter's\ndefinition, and the two are kept apart. The theorem is the seal of the computation\n(the second capstone), stated over the tape the emitter renders:\nevery leaf in the backward cone of an output is either a named input or a baked constant the\nhypergraph's assumption ledger lists, two environments that agree on the influencers of an output\ngive the same value at it, and every edge between observable vertices realizes one hop of one\noccurrence the source states, with the kinds that occurrence names — the kind-transporting weak\nbisimulation between the metrological provenance hypergraph and the computational tape graph. The\nreading is of genJulia: it adds no entry point to that picture — a Julia argument exists for\nexactly the named leaves (its input list is collectInputs, the function gen uses, name for\nname), a literal appears in the body for exactly the baked leaves (rendered from the double's bit\npattern, so it is the constant the ledger lists), and a line of the body is one node's operation\non its parents, over the alphabet the Float interpreter cOp defines. That reading is a theorem,\nand not a theorem about Julia: every emitter is a printer of one backend-neutral straight-line\nprogram that genSL — the only function that reads the tape — produces, one line per node, and\nthe denotation of the rendering says the program runs as the tape evaluator,\nnode for node, with a literal exactly at a baked leaf and an input read exactly at a named leaf\n(genSL_denotes, genSL_lit_iff, genSL_input_iff), and that the program's graph is the tape's\n(ofProg_eq_ofTape), so the seal of the computation is a theorem about the program a host receives\n(semantic_seal_program). The Julia printer (printJulia) is a total function\nfrom a program line to text that cannot see the tape, so it cannot add an input, a literal or an\noperation the program does not have; what it leaves to trust is that each of its dozen forms means\nin Julia what the line means in Lean's Float. In the words a reviewer would use: no naked number\nenters the Julia code beyond what the algorithm's contract admits — and the same sentence holds,\nby the same theorem, of the C stub and the CUDA kernel, which print the same program. An input the hypergraph says cannot reach an output\ndoes not reach it in the Julia function either, because the Julia function is the tape, and the\nstatement was proved of the tape.\n\nWhat the emitter pins, so the generated file is in the regime the theorems describe (the header of\nevery emitted file says the same):\n\n* no @fastmath, no muladd — Julia does not contract a·b + c to a fused multiply-add or\n  re-associate unless asked, which is the nvcc --fmad=false regime the megakernel is compiled in;* min/max are written out as ifelse(a <= b, a, b) / ifelse(a <= b, b, a), which is Lean's\n  Min Float/Max Float (minOfLe/maxOfLe) to the letter, NaN cases included — the same\n  ≤-ternary the C and CUDA renderings write, so the interpreter and all three renderings are one\n  function on NaN inputs (fminf/fmaxf, which return the non-NaN operand, are not used);* sqrt and log of a negative argument throw in Julia where IEEE returns a quiet NaN; the emitted\n  helpers restore the NaN and are the identity elsewhere;* precision is a type parameter T: at Float64 the emitted function is bit-identical to evalTape\n  on the IEEE-exact alphabet, because Lean's Float is binary64 — the first emitted target with no\n  narrowing at the boundary, where the CUDA kernel is float with double ↔ float32 casts in its\n  launcher; at Float32 it is the device kernel's precision.\n\nWhat it does not do, said plainly. It does not put the host's own code under the seal: the Julia\nmodel's dynamical core, time stepping and communication are the host's, and the seal says nothing\nabout them. It does not certify Julia's compiler or its exp/log: those are the trusted base the\ntarget adds, named as such — LLVM compiling a straight-line function, and a third libm (pure-Julia\nBase.Math) beside glibc's and CUDA's. The honest check across that base is apps.julia_parity, and its\nscope is the three recorded tapes it carries — the AVS residual and Jacobian, the whole projected\nLM step, and the table-fetch model of examples.tape_codegen_lut — not every kernel the emitter\ncould be given: each emitted file is run under julia at Float64 over deterministic pixels\nagainst the tape evaluator, bit-identical on every output whose cone has no exp/log, within an\nulp bound on the outputs whose cone does. A new closure earns the same check by being added as a\ncase.\nAnd it covers the tape's program class and no more — a branchless, fixed-shape computational tape graph over\nten operations and a table fetch, with reductions only when unrolled — so a closure with a\ndata-dependent loop, a runtime-size reduction or a stencil is outside it today; the first such\nclosure recorded against [NumCarrier α] is what tells which operation to add to the alphabet, each\nwith its interpreter case, its two renderings and its rendering-table row.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Write Once, Correctly\u0009Go fast, automatically — the deployment spectrum","header":"18.3.7. Julia, emitted — the sealed algorithm handed to a host that cannot call a theorem","id":"/Write-Once___-Correctly/Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum--Julia___-emitted-___-the-sealed-algorithm-handed-to-a-host-that-cannot-call-a-theorem"}});